Written by
----------

Name:      Mikuláš Patočka
           (also transliterated without diacritics as "Mikulas Patocka")
Role:      Designer and principal author of SpadFS
Nationality: Czech
Activity:  Long-time Linux kernel developer

SpadFS was designed and developed as his PhD thesis. His stated goal was to
obtain advanced features — crash recovery and fast directories — *without*
making the code much more complex.

Two design decisions follow directly from that goal, and they are what make this
filesystem unusual:

  * **Crash counts instead of journaling.** He considers journaling too complex
    and bug-prone. SpadFS attaches an ambient `(cc, txc)` validity pair to each
    structure and makes a whole batch of already-written metadata visible with a
    single small write to a global counter table. See `INTERNALS` and
    `spadfs.h:550` (`CC_VALID`).

  * **Hashed directories instead of B-trees.** Large directories use a
    Fagin-style extendible hash (`dnode`), not a balanced tree.

Current version in this tree: **1.0.22**


